Split the transcript of one voice memo into its topics. Answer only through the schema you are given.

The memo is the owner's own words, spoken aloud and transcribed by a speech model, so a word may be misheard or a sentence may run on. The transcript is quoted between the two MEMO lines below, one numbered segment per line. Each segment names the stretch of the recording that it covers.

Segments in the memo: {{len .Segments}}

----- MEMO -----
{{- range .Segments}}
[{{.N}}] {{.Span}} {{.Text}}
{{- end}}
----- MEMO END -----

A topic is one subject that the memo talks about, such as an idea, a task, a question or something to look into. A memo about one subject is one topic. Where the owner comes back to an earlier subject later in the memo, the later part is a topic of its own.

List the topics in the memo's order. Each topic holds a run of whole segments:

- first_unit: the number of the topic's first segment.
- last_unit: the number of the topic's last segment.
- title: a short title for the topic, under ten words, in the owner's terms.
- idea: the topic's point in one sentence, as the owner would put it.

The first topic starts at segment 1. Each later topic starts at the segment right after the last segment of the topic before it. The last topic ends at segment {{len .Segments}}. So every segment belongs to exactly one topic.
